## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a Kubernetes-native control plane for GPU-backed ML workloads, balancing quotas, preemption, and data access while preserving isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes Operators and CRDs
- GPU scheduling with taints, tolerations, and resource requests
- Priority, preemption and multi-tenant quotas
- Data locality and streaming of dataset shards
- Observability, auditing, and lifecycle finalizers

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler loop for GpuTrainJob
while (true) {
  const jobs = listGpuTrainJobs();
  for (const j of jobs) {
    if (!quotaOk(j)) continue;
    if (!podExists(j)) {
      createGpuPod(j); // with nodeSelector for j.gpuType and resources
    }
    updateStatus(j, {phase: "Running"});
  }
  await sleep(1000);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate preemption scenarios in tests?
- How would you handle mixed GPU architectures and driver versions in the same cluster?

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced latency management in multi-tenant clusters requires concrete CRD-driven control with observable outcomes and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes CRD and controller reconciliation loops
- Prometheus-based latency metrics (p95) and windowing
- Mitigations: HPA scaling, admission throttling, proxy-sidecar rate limits
- Status, events, auditability, and rollback semantics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: reconciliation skeleton
function reconcile(spec, status) {
  const latency = queryPrometheus(spec.namespace, spec.serviceSelector, 'p95');
  if (latency > spec.budgetMs) {
    scaleOut(spec.namespace, spec.serviceSelector);
    enableRateLimit(spec.sidecarSvc, spec.budgetMs);
  } else {
    removeRateLimit(spec.sidecarSvc);
  }
  return { observedLatency: latency, breached: latency > spec.budgetMs };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test canary deployments for this policy?
- What safety checks would you implement to avoid flapping or service disruption?

